    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Proof. To prove that (1) implies (2), we assume that i∗1R0 = 0. Recall that bJ is a cocycle representing the Reznikov class. Then there exists u ∈ C1(G) such that i∗1bJ = δu. Since bJ is a bounded cocycle, u is a quasimorphism. Recall from (2.4) that −δνJ = p∗i∗0i∗1bJ. Hence we have"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.672, 260.155, 485.331, 336.183 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1095",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "−δνJ = p∗i∗0δu = δ(p∗i∗0u) = δ( i0∗p∗u). This implies that νJ + i0∗p∗u: Hamc(M,ω) → R is a homomorphism. Because Hamc(M,ω) is perfect ([Ban78]), we have νJ = − i0∗p∗u. Let ϕ be the homogenization of −u. Then we have SM = i0∗p∗ϕ."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.672, 216.934, 485.331, 267.953 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1095",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "To prove that (2) implies (1), we assume (2) and take ϕ ∈ Q(G) satisfying i0∗p∗ϕ = SM. Since SM is the homogenization of νJ, there exists a bounded function v: Hamc(M,ω) → R such that SM = νJ + v. Then we have"
  }, {
